1. Check Ansible version

First, check whether Ansible is installed:

ansible --version

This confirms that Ansible is available in your WSL/Linux environment.

2. Check available collections

You can search for collections using Ansible Galaxy.

For example, if you want to work with AWS:

ansible-galaxy collection search amazon.aws

You will see information about the amazon.aws collection.

3. Install a Collection

Install the AWS collection:

ansible-galaxy collection install amazon.aws

Ansible will download and install the collection.

You may see output similar to:

Starting galaxy collection install process
Process install dependency map
Starting collection install process
Downloading collection 'amazon.aws'
Installing 'amazon.aws'

This means the collection has been successfully installed.

4. Verify the Collection

To check installed collections:

ansible-galaxy collection list

You should see something similar to:

# /home/username/.ansible/collections/ansible_collections
Collection    Version
------------  -------
amazon.aws    x.x.x

The exact version may be different.

5. Understand where the Collection is installed

By default, user-installed collections are commonly stored under:

~/.ansible/collections/ansible_collections/

Inside it, you may find:

ansible_collections/
└── amazon/
    └── aws/
        ├── plugins/
        ├── roles/
        ├── docs/
        └── ...

You don't normally need to manually modify these files.

6. Check Collection Documentation

You can get information about a collection with:

ansible-galaxy collection list

You can also use the Ansible documentation to understand the modules available in the collection.

For example, AWS collection modules can include functionality for:

EC2
S3
VPC
Security Groups
IAM
and other AWS services

7. Use a Collection in a Playbook

You can explicitly mention the collection in your playbook:

---
- name: AWS Collection Example
  hosts: localhost
  connection: local

  collections:
    - amazon.aws

  tasks:
    - name: Example AWS task
      ...

The important part here is:

collections:
  - amazon.aws

It tells Ansible:

"I want to use modules from the amazon.aws collection."

8. Better approach: Use FQCN

Modern Ansible playbooks commonly use the Fully Qualified Collection Name (FQCN) directly.

For example:

amazon.aws.ec2_instance

Instead of just:

ec2_instance

This makes it clear which collection the module belongs to.

9. Example with AWS EC2

A simplified example looks like this:

---
- name: Create EC2 Instance
  hosts: localhost
  connection: local

  tasks:
    - name: Create EC2 instance
      amazon.aws.ec2_instance:
        name: my-test-server
        instance_type: t2.micro
        image_id: ami-xxxxxxxx
        key_name: my-key
        region: ap-south-1

Important: ami-xxxxxxxx is only an example. For an actual lab, you need a valid AMI ID for your AWS region.

10. What happens when you run the playbook?

When you run:

ansible-playbook ec2.yml

Ansible roughly does this:

Playbook
   ↓
amazon.aws collection
   ↓
ec2_instance module
   ↓
AWS API
   ↓
EC2 Instance

So Ansible communicates with AWS using the functionality provided by the collection.

11. Collection Dependencies

Some collections require other collections or Python packages to work properly.

For example, AWS automation may require the appropriate AWS Python SDK on the machine running Ansible.

This is why collections often have dependencies.

You can think of it like:

Your Playbook
     ↓
Collection
     ↓
Required dependencies
     ↓
AWS

12. Useful Commands for the Lab

a) Search for a collection
ansible-galaxy collection search amazon.aws

b) Install a collection
ansible-galaxy collection install amazon.aws

c) List installed collections
ansible-galaxy collection list

d) Check Ansible version
ansible --version

e) Run your playbook
ansible-playbook ec2.yml
